<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Corippus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Flavius Cresconius Corippus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Flavius Cresconius Corippus was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poet from Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> in the 6th century. His epic Iohannis, in eight books, celebrates the campaigns of the Byzantine general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-troglita">John Troglita</a> against the Moorish tribes in the 540s and is a chief source for the Laguatan and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> peoples. He later wrote a panegyric on the accession of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-ii">Justin II</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/corippus.jpg" alt="Corippus" width="320" height="584" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christophori Plantini · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corippi_Africani_grammatici_De_laudibus_Iustini_Augusti.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Corippus's full name is known from a single document, and nothing is known of his life beyond what his own poems tell. A native of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, he is called africanus grammaticus in one medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, and he has sometimes been identified, on insufficient grounds, with Cresconius Africanus, a Catholic bishop who compiled a collection of church law. He appears to have served as a tribune or notary under Anastasius, treasurer and chamberlain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>, and late in that emperor's reign he left Africa for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, apparently after losing his property in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandalic-war">Vandalic War</a> and the Moorish revolts that followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Iohannis, also called De Bellis Libycis, was dedicated to the nobles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. In eight books, the last unfinished, it follows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-troglita">John Troglita</a>, magister militum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, from his dispatch by Justinian to his decisive victory near Carthage in 548. Its picture of the land and peoples of late Roman Africa is that of an observer from the province, and many of its statements on customs are confirmed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> and by what is known of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>.</p><p>In laudem Iustini minoris, in four books and written when Corippus was old, tells of the death of Justinian, the coronation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-ii">Justin II</a> on 13 November 565 and the reception of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> embassy, and throws much light on court ceremony, though critics have found it servile. He also wrote a panegyric for Anastasius, then quaestor and master of offices.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Corippus took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>, Lucan and Claudian as his models, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> is counted unusually pure for his age; he is probably the last important Latin author of late antiquity. The absence of mythological machinery and his allusions to Scripture suggest he was a Christian. The Iohannis survived in a single full copy made in the late 14th century by Giovanni De Bonis and found in 1814 in the Trivulzio library near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Corippus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>What is known comes from Procopius and <b>Corippus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-capsus">Kingdom of Capsus</a></li><li><blockquote>The confederation was led by chiefs, and its leader Ierna was also priest of the god Gurzil, whose image was carried into battle, according to the Latin poet <b>Corippus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/laguatan">Laguatan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.44,9.68&amp;year=564" aria-label="North Africa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4801 1053 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Corippus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5251 1323h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5251 1323h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.44,9.68&amp;year=564">What was here in 564 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
